{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f897ed322acfabe", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAudio Engineering Society\n2\t\n3\tConference Paper\n4\tPresented at the AES International Conference on\n5\tArtificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio\n6\t2025 September 8–10, London, UK\n7\tThis paper was peer-reviewed as a complete manuscript for presentation at this conference. This paper is available in the AES\n8\tE-Library (http://www.aes.org/e-lib), all rights reserved. Reproduction of this paper, or any portion thereof, is not permitted\n9\twithout direct permission from the Journal of the Audio Engineering Society.\n10\t\n11\tAutomatic Audio Equalization with Semantic Embeddings\n12\t\n13\tarXiv:2607.23846v1 [cs.SD] 26 Jul 2026\n14\t\n15\tEloi Moliner1 , Vesa Välimäki1 , Konstantinos Drossos2 , and Matti S. Hämäläinen2\n16\t1 Acoustics Lab, Department of Information and Communications Engineering, Aalto University, Espoo, Finland\n17\t2 Nokia Technologies, Tampere/Espoo, Finland\n18\t\n19\tCorrespondence should be addressed to Eloi Moliner (eloi.moliner@aalto.fi)\n20\t\n21\tABSTRACT\n22\t\n23\tThis paper presents a data-driven approach to automatic blind equalization of audio by predicting log-mel spectral\n24\tfeatures and deriving an inverse filter. The method uses a deep neural network, where a pre-trained model provides\n25\tsemantic embeddings as a backbone, and only a lightweight head is trained. This design is intended to enhance\n26\ttraining efficiency and generalization. Trained on both music and speech, the model is robust to noise and\n27\treverberation. Objective evaluations confirm its effectiveness, and subjective tests show performance comparable\n28\tto that of an oracle that uses true log-mel spectral features, indicating that the model accurately estimates the\n29\tdesired characteristics, with remaining limitations attributed to the filtering stage. Overall, the results highlight the\n30\tpotential of the method for real-world audio enhancement applications.\n31\t\n32\t1\n33\t\n34\tIntroduction\n35\t\n36\tAutomatic blind equalization (EQ) of speech and music is a key aspect in audio processing. The goal is\n37\tto adjust the spectrum of an audio signal to achieve a\n38\tdesired tonal balance without knowledge of the original\n39\trecording conditions or the target equalization curve [1].\n40\tSuch processing enhances the audio listening experience by improving clarity and consistency. By enabling\n41\tdata-driven adjustments, EQ can significantly improve\n42\tsound quality across a wide range of contexts. Automatic equalization is used in music production [2, 3],\n43\thearing aids [4], and teleconferencing [5].\n44\tThis problem has been studied extensively. A wellknown approach involves estimating an equalization\n45\tcurve by averaging the spectral characteristics of a reference recording or collection of recordings [6, 7]. More\n46\trecently, deep learning techniques have been explored,\n47\t\n48\temploying neural networks to model complex exampledependent spectral transformations. End-to-end convolutional networks have been applied to automatic\n49\tEQ [8], while methods based on self-supervised frameworks have been proposed for blind parameter estimation [9, 10, 11]. Closer to this work, Mockenhaupt et al.\n50\t[12] estimated EQ targets using a classification-based\n51\tapproach, though the reliance on manually defined targets limits flexibility. Generative models have also been\n52\texplored, with Moliner et al. applying a diffusion-based\n53\tmodel for equalization and audio restoration [13].\n54\tWhile deep learning has shown promise for EQ, practical deployment requires models that generalize across\n55\tdiverse signals and remain robust to noise and reverberation. This paper presents a data-driven approach to\n56\tblind equalization by predicting log-mel spectral features and deriving an inverse filter, explicitly optimized\n57\tfor robustness and generalization.\n58\t\n59\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n60\t\n61\tAutomatic Audio Equalization\n62\t\n63\tThis paper is organized as follows. Sec. 2 defines the\n64\tblind EQ problem as an inverse filtering task. Sec. 3\n65\tdetails the proposed method, which combines a pretrained feature extractor with a lightweight trainable\n66\thead for parameter estimation. Sec. 4 describes the\n67\texperimentals, including training on speech and music.\n68\tSecs. 5 and 6 present objective and subjective evaluations. Finally, Sec. 7 summarizes key findings.\n69\t\n70\t2\n71\t\n72\tProblem Definition\n73\t\n74\tThe problem is how to automatically equalize the tonal\n75\tbalance of a degraded audio signal towards a reference.\n76\tLet x ∈ RL represent a high-quality reference audio\n77\tsignal. We observe a degraded version of this signal,\n78\ty ∈ RL , generated through the forward model:\n79\ty = A(x) + n,\n80\t\n81\t(1)\n82\t\n83\twhere A(·) : RL → RL is a degradation operator (e.g.,\n84\tfiltering or nonlinear distortion), and n ∈ RL captures\n85\tadditive noise from environmental interference, equipment limitations, or other sources. Our objective is to\n86\testimate a reconstruction x̂ ≈ x. Assuming that A(·) is\n87\ta linear time-invariant system, the degradation can be\n88\tmodeled as a convolution with a finite impulse response\n89\tfilter h ∈ RK of length K:\n90\tA(x) ≈ h ∗ x,\n91\t\n92\t(2)\n93\t\n94\twhere ∗ denotes discrete convolution. We further assume that the problem is well-conditioned, implying\n95\tthat the filter is invertible, with inverse h−1 . In the\n96\tnoiseless case, the reference can be approximated as\n97\tx ≈ x̂ = ĥ−1 ∗ y.\n98\t\n99\t(3)\n100\t\n101\tThus, the task of estimating the reference is reduced\n102\tto estimating the inverse filter ĥ−1 . However, this approximation does not hold in the presence of noise\n103\tn. As we will explain later, this issue is addressed by\n104\tpre-processing y with a denoiser. In cases where the\n105\tproblem is ill-conditioned, such as when portions of\n106\tthe signal (e.g., certain frequency bands or segments)\n107\tare lost or severely degraded, our method is limited to\n108\trestoring only the nondegraded parts of the signal.\n109\t\n110\t3\n111\t\n112\tMethods\n113\t\n114\t3.1\n115\t\n116\tApproximating the Inverse Filter\n117\t\n118\tThis work aims to estimate the inverse filter h−1\n119\ti without any prior knowledge of the forward filter hi , making\n120\t\n121\tit a blind estimation task. The approach is performed in\n122\tthe frequency domain, where we estimate the inverse\n123\tfilter Ĥ−1 ∈ CNFFT , based on a chosen FFT size NFFT .\n124\tEquation 3 can be written in the frequency domain as\n125\t\u0001\n126\tx ≈ F −1 Ĥ−1 ⊙ F(y) ,\n127\t(4)\n128\t\n129\twhere F(·) and F −1 (·) denote the short-time Fourier\n130\ttransform (STFT) and its inverse (iSTFT), respectively.\n131\tBoth transformations are computed using NFFT frequency bins, with a window size and hop size defined in Sec. 4.3. Owing to the Hermitian symmetry\n132\tof the Fourier transform for real-valued signals, only\n133\tNFFT /2 + 1 bins contain non-redundant information,\n134\tand thus only these need to be estimated in practice.\n135\tWe constrain the inverse filter Ĥ−1 to be a zero-phase\n136\tfilter, and approximate its magnitude as\n137\ts\n138\tX2ref\n139\t|Ĥ−1 | =\n140\t,\n141\t(5)\n142\tY2avg\n143\twhere Y2avg is the time-averaged Power Spectral Density (PSD) of the observed signal y, computed as\n144\tY2avg =\n145\t\n146\t1 M−1\n147\t∑ |STFT(y)m |2 .\n148\tM m=0\n149\t\n150\t(6)\n151\t\n152\tHere, M is the number of STFT frames, and X2ref denotes the target or reference spectral shape, which is\n153\ttypically content-dependent. The reference X2ref could\n154\tbe a user-defined spectral curve, or, more generally, the\n155\taverage power spectrum of the unknown clean source\n156\tx, given in an analogous way as in Eq. (6).\n157\tSince the clean source x is unknown, we estimate the\n158\ttarget spectrum X̂2ref ≈ X2ref using a deep neural network\n159\t(DNN). The DNN denoted as Eθ (y) is trained to predict\n160\tthe target spectrum given the observed signal y, where\n161\tθ represents the trainable parameters of the network.\n162\tInstead of predicting the target spectral shape directly\n163\tX̂2ref , we reduce its dimensionality by using a mel-scale\n164\ttriangular filterbank. The mel-transformed reference\n165\tspectrum zref ∈ RNmel is given by\n166\tzref = 10 log10 (MX2ref ),\n167\t\n168\t(7)\n169\t\n170\twhere M ∈ RNmel ×(NFFT /2+1) represents the mel-scale\n171\tfilterbank, which compresses the non-redundant frequency bins into fewer mel-scaled bins Nmel . The\n172\t\n173\tAES International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio, London, UK\n174\t2025 September 8–10\n175\tPage 2 of 11\n176\t\n177\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n178\t\n179\tAutomatic Audio Equalization\n180\t\n181\tDNN is trained to predict the features zref , such that\n182\tEθ (y) = ẑ ≈ zref .\n183\tOnce we have obtained ẑ, the estimated time-averaged\n184\tPSD in the original frequency domain can be computed\n185\tby reversing the mel transformation:\n186\tX̂2ref = MT 10ẑ/10 .\n187\t\n188\t(8)\n189\t\n190\tHere, MT is the transpose of the mel filterbank, which\n191\tinterpolates the full FFT frequency resolution from the\n192\tlower-dimensional mel representation.\n193\t3.2\n194\t\n195\tBlind Parameter Estimation\n196\t\n197\tThe DNN model Eθ is designed to estimate the logmel spectral features z(x) of an original recording x,\n198\tgiven a processed observation y. The target parameters z(x) are extracted from the clean signal x using a\n199\tprocedure described in Sec. 3.1. During training, the\n200\tobservation y is generated by applying a forward transformation f (x) to the same original recording. This\n201\ttransformation introduces a range of perturbations, including randomized spectral coloration, variations in\n202\tloudness, room reverberation, and additive noise. This\n203\tmakes the spectral parameter estimation non-trivial.\n204\tWe hypothesize that z(x), being both time-averaged\n205\tand compressed through a mel filterbank, is correlated\n206\twith the type of content in the input signal. For example, orchestral music tends to have a distinct spectral\n207\tenvelope, while speech has a different one, and characteristics like gender or age may further affect the spectral shape of speech signals. Based on this, the model\n208\tEθ (y) is expected to extract semantic information from\n209\tthe processed signal y and map it to the corresponding\n210\tlog-mel spectral features ẑ.\n211\tTo achieve this, we propose factorizing the DNN model\n212\tEθ into two components, such that\n213\tEθ (y) = (MLPθMLP ◦ CLAPθCLAP )(y),\n214\t\n215\t(9)\n216\t\n217\twhere CLAPθCLAP is a pretrained audio encoder [14],\n218\tand MLPθMLP is a small multi-layer perceptron (MLP)\n219\tthat learns the mapping from Contrastive Audio Language Pretraining (CLAP) embeddings to log-mel spectral features. CLAP is a joint audio-text encoder trained\n220\tvia contrastive learning, mapping audio and text pairs\n221\tinto a shared embedding space. We use the audio encoder of CLAP, with its parameters frozen during training, to extract semantic features from the signal y.\n222\t\n223\tPrevious work has shown that embeddings from CLAP\n224\tand similar models often fail to capture the details of\n225\taudio effects and transformations, such as those introduced by spectral processing, limiting their sensitivity\n226\tto these changes [15, 16]. This is beneficial in our case,\n227\tas it suggests that the forward operator f (·), which\n228\tintroduces spectral coloration, may not significantly\n229\taffect CLAP-derived embeddings. Thus, MLPθMLP can\n230\tfocus on learning the relationship between the extracted\n231\tembeddings and the log-mel spectral features.\n232\tThe parameters of z(x) typically exhibit varying magnitudes, which generally decrease logarithmically as a\n233\tfunction of frequency. Normalizing both the input and\n234\ttarget data is beneficial during neural network training,\n235\tas it ensures consistent scaling and improves model\n236\tconvergence. To achieve uniform error impact across\n237\tall frequency bands, we first compute the average zavg\n238\tcoefficients from a representative subset of the training\n239\tdata. The model is then trained to estimate the deviation\n240\tof each instance from this average, ensuring balanced\n241\tattention across the different frequency components.\n242\tThe parameter estimation model Eθ is optimized using\n243\tthe following loss function:\n244\t\u0002\n245\t\u0003\n246\tL = Ex∼px , f (·)∼p f ∥Eθ ( f (x)) − (z(x) − zavg )∥1 ,\n247\t(10)\n248\twhere x represents clean audio data sampled from a\n249\tdistribution px (e.g., a dataset of recordings), and the\n250\tforward operator f (·) is sampled from a distribution p f ,\n251\trepresenting different types of data augmentations. As\n252\tillustrated in Fig. 3, these degradations include room\n253\treverberation, randomized spectral coloration, additive\n254\tnoise, and gain randomization. Importantly, since the\n255\tCLAP encoder is frozen during training, only the parameters of the MLP θMLP ⊂ θ are optimized based\n256\ton the loss objective. It is important to note that, due\n257\tto the ℓ1 -norm minimization objective, at convergence\n258\tthe network Eθ approximates the conditional median\n259\tof z(x) given the transformed input f (x) under the\n260\ttraining data distribution. Thus, the estimated power\n261\tspectrum ẑ = Eθ ( f (x)) can be interpreted as\n262\tẑ ≈ medianz∼p(z| f (x)) [z − zavg ] + zavg ,\n263\t\n264\t(11)\n265\t\n266\twhere the median is computed implicitly over the target\n267\tdistribution defined by the training dataset.\n268\tThe training process is illustrated in Fig. 1, and the\n269\tsignal processing conducted at inference time is summarized in Fig. 2. For simplicity, the detail of zavg is\n270\tomitted in both Fig. 1 and 2.\n271\t\n272\tAES International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio, London, UK\n273\t2025 September 8–10\n274\tPage 3 of 11\n275\t\n276\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n277\t\n278\tx\n279\t\n280\tAutomatic Audio Equalization\n281\t\n282\tnoise n\n283\t\n284\ty\n285\t\n286\tf (·)\n287\t\n288\tx\n289\t\n290\tnoise n\n291\tAvg. Power\n292\tSpectrum\n293\t\n294\tRIR\n295\th(t)\n296\t\n297\tRandom EQ\n298\tfilter H(f )\n299\t\n300\tRandom RMS\n301\tnormalization\n302\t\n303\ty\n304\t\n305\tCLAP\n306\t\n307\tFig. 3: Pipeline illustrating the applied degradations.\n308\tMLP\n309\t\n310\tMel filterbank\n311\tz\n312\t\n313\tL1 loss\n314\t\n315\tẑ\n316\t\n317\tFig. 1: Training diagram.\n318\ty\n319\t\n320\tEnhancement\n321\t(optional)\n322\t\n323\tAvg. Power\n324\tSpectrum\n325\t\n326\tCLAP\n327\tF −1\n328\t\n329\tMLP\n330\t\n331\tẑ\n332\t\n333\tMel interp.\n334\t\n335\t2\n336\tYavg\n337\t\n338\tr\n339\t\n340\tX̂2avg\n341\t2\n342\tYavg\n343\t\n344\tF(y)\n345\t\n346\t!\n347\t\n348\tx̂\n349\t\n350\tX̂2ref\n351\t\n352\tFig. 2: Inference diagram.\n353\t\n354\t4\n355\t\n356\tExperiments\n357\t\n358\t4.1\n359\t\n360\tTraining Data Pipeline\n361\t\n362\tWe develop a single model capable of processing both\n363\tspeech and music. The model is designed to exhibit\n364\trobustness to background noise, gain variations, and\n365\treverberation, necessitating the inclusion of these disturbances within the training data pipeline. We utilize\n366\tthe speech datasets VCTK [17] and EARS [18], both of\n367\twhich consist of high-quality studio speech recordings\n368\tsampled at 48 kHz, and which overall contain around\n369\t200 different speakers.\n370\tWe choose MedleyDB [19] and DSD100 [20] as the\n371\tmusic datasets, both offering studio-quality recordings\n372\tacross various genres such as rock, pop, classical, and\n373\tjazz. These datasets are mixed by recording engineers,\n374\tallowing us to consider the spectral balance as an appropriate target. Although the datasets provide isolated\n375\tstems for every source in the microphone, we limit our\n376\tuse to the mixed tracks. Every audio segment undergoes loudness normalization to achieve a standardized\n377\tvolume level of −18 LUFS. This procedure guarantees\n378\ta consistent loudness target, ensuring uniformity across\n379\tdifferent audio files.\n380\tThe degradation process is outlined in Fig. 3, illustrating the practical implementation of f (·) as shown in\n381\t\n382\tFig. 1. Initially, an audio segment with approximately\n383\t7 s in duration, containing either speech or music, is\n384\tconvolved with a room impulse response (RIR). We\n385\tspecifically use RIRs from the Arni dataset [21]. Subsequently, the audio is subjected to an equalization\n386\tfilter featuring random gains across various frequency\n387\tbands. This filter is constructed in the frequency domain as a zero-phase FIR filter. The gain for a set of\n388\t26 bark-scaled frequency bands is stochastically sampled on a log-uniform scale ranging from -20 to 20\n389\tdB. In the next phase, noise is introduced. For noise\n390\tsources, the TAU Urban Acoustic Scenes dataset [22]\n391\tis employed during training. This dataset is integrated\n392\twith speech and music by mixing with noise from this\n393\tdataset, achieving an SNR that is log-uniformly sampled between 5 and 50 dB.\n394\tFinally, the gain of the mixture is adjusted through\n395\ta random root-mean-square (RMS) normalization.\n396\tThe√ RMS normalization is implemented as y =\n397\t(α L/∥x∥2 )x, with the parameter α being uniformly\n398\tsampled within −5 dB and 30 dB.\n399\t4.2\n400\t\n401\tTraining Details\n402\t\n403\tThe training of the model consisted of conducting\n404\t100,000 iterations, utilizing the Adam optimization\n405\talgorithm. The learning rate employed during this process was set to 1 × 10−4 , and the values of the beta momentum parameters were configured to be (0.9, 0.999).\n406\tFor the training data, audio segments of 7 s sampled at\n407\t48 kHz were used. The training was performed with a\n408\tbatch size of 16.\n409\tWe chose a number of mel-frequency coefficients\n410\tNmel = 32, extracted using the STFT with FFT size\n411\t2048, a Hann window with size 1024, and a hop length\n412\tof 256 samples. The employed MLP contains 0.34 M\n413\tparameters and is based on three linear layers with a\n414\thidden size of 256 and Leaky-ReLU nonlinearities.\n415\t4.3\n416\t\n417\tEvaluation Data\n418\t\n419\tThe model is evaluated on speech and music, and in different scenarios, according to the applied degradations.\n420\t\n421\tAES International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio, London, UK\n422\t2025 September 8–10\n423\tPage 4 of 11\n424\t\n425\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n426\t\n427\tWe distinguish the following scenarios: 1. Speech\n428\tequalization, 2. Music equalization, 3. Noisy speech\n429\tequalization, and 4. Reverberant speech equalization.\n430\tFor scenarios 1, 3, and 4, we evaluate on VCTK\n431\tspeakers “p351”, “p360”–“p364”, “p374”, “p376” and\n432\tEARS speakers “p100”–“p107”. In scenario 2, we\n433\textract 8-s segments from separated test splits of MedleyDB and DSD100. For each of these scenarios, a\n434\ttest set comprising 1,000 samples is created. To each\n435\texample, a random filter is applied following the same\n436\tprocedure used during training, and the gain is randomized with an RMS in the interval [0.005, 0.30],\n437\textending the range seen at training time.\n438\tIn scenario 3, we introduce noise from the DEMAND\n439\tdataset [23] and apply SNRs ranging between −5 dB\n440\tand 30 dB, which also extends beyond the training\n441\trange. Finally, in scenario 4, the signals are convolved\n442\twith RIRs, utilizing simulated RIRs from pyroomacoustics [24], with a reverberation time T60 spanning\n443\t[0.1, 1.0]. In both scenarios, we apply DeepFilterNet2\n444\t[25] to pre-process audio signals that are either noisy\n445\tor reverberant. DeepFilterNet2 was selected due to\n446\tits open-source availability, lightweight architecture,\n447\thigh processing speed, and acceptable performance. It\n448\tis important to note that the original signal remains\n449\taccessible to the model, while the pre-processing is\n450\tintegrated into the equalization pipeline, as shown in\n451\tFig. 2.\n452\t4.4\n453\t\n454\tMetrics\n455\t\n456\tTo evaluate the performance of the model, we consider\n457\tseveral metrics that provide a comprehensive analysis\n458\tof its effectiveness in estimating the power spectrum\n459\tand enhancing audio signals. These metrics capture\n460\tboth spectral accuracy and audio-domain fidelity.\n461\tWe compute the L1 error between the target average\n462\tpower spectrum extracted from the reference highquality recording and the estimated power spectrum.\n463\tThis metric corresponds directly to the loss function\n464\tused during training, as defined in Eq. (10). It provides\n465\ta measure of how closely the model’s predictions align\n466\twith the target power spectrum, reflecting its ability to\n467\tlearn accurate spectral representations.\n468\tTo evaluate the fidelity of the equalized audio signal,\n469\twe employ the Log-Spectral Distortion (LSD), which\n470\tmeasures differences in spectral features between the\n471\t\n472\tAutomatic Audio Equalization\n473\t\n474\treference and predicted audio signals. The LSD is\n475\tcomputed as\n476\t1\n477\t∑ log(|F (x)| + ε) − log(|F (x̂)| + ε) ,\n478\tMK m,k\n479\t(12)\n480\twhere F represents the Short-Time Fourier Transform\n481\t(STFT) operator, M is the number of time frames, K is\n482\tthe number of frequency bins, and ε is a small constant\n483\t(e.g., 10−6 ) to avoid logarithmic instabilities.\n484\tLSD =\n485\t\n486\tWhile these objective metrics are useful for evaluating model performance, they are not always directly\n487\tindicative of perceptual quality. For example, the equalization applied to music examples may deviate from\n488\tthe median power spectrum the model predicts. This\n489\tis because the model’s estimation inherently reflects\n490\tthe statistical properties of the training data, whereas\n491\treal-world audio signals often exhibit unique spectral\n492\tcharacteristics. Thus, while the metrics provide quantitative insights, qualitative evaluation through listening\n493\ttests is essential to fully assess the method’s impact on\n494\tperceptual audio quality.\n495\t4.5\n496\t\n497\tBaselines\n498\t\n499\tTo contextualize the performance of our proposed\n500\tmethod, we designed and evaluate the proposed method\n501\tagainst several baselines. The first baseline, which we\n502\tcall Average, computes the average power spectrum of\n503\tthe training data, and uses that to compute the inverse\n504\tfilter in Eq. (5). We compute the average of the speech\n505\tdata and music data separately. By using these fixed\n506\taverage spectra as predictions, this baseline is meant to\n507\thighlight the effectiveness of the proposed method at\n508\tleveraging data-specific patterns compared to a naive\n509\tglobal estimate.\n510\tThe second baseline, which we call End-to-end, replaces the frozen CLAP encoder with a fully trainable\n511\tencoder, allowing the model to jointly optimize feature\n512\textraction and power spectrum estimation. We used the\n513\tsame neural network architecture as in the setup from\n514\tSteinmetz et al. [9]. This baseline aims to demonstrate\n515\tthe advantages of using a pre-trained representation\n516\tover task-specific feature learning.\n517\tFinally, the Oracle baseline uses parameters directly\n518\testimated from the reference high-quality audio signal,\n519\tbypassing the need for a model prediction. This serves\n520\tas an upper bound on performance within our framework, illustrating the best possible outcome achievable\n521\twhen the target parameters are perfectly known.\n522\t\n523\tAES International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio, London, UK\n524\t2025 September 8–10\n525\tPage 5 of 11\n526\t\n527\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n528\t\n529\t5\n530\t\n531\tObjective Evaluation\n532\t\n533\t5.1\n534\t\n535\tSpeech Equalization and Gain Adjustment\n536\t\n537\tIn this scenario, speech signals undergo arbitrary EQ\n538\tand random RMS processing. The outcomes are depicted in Fig. 4. Each plot displays the median curves\n539\trepresented by colored lines, with the InterQuartile\n540\tRange (25%-75%) illustrated as shaded regions. Figure 4a illustrates L1 errors on parameter estimation\n541\tin relation to frequency, highlighting increased errors\n542\tin higher frequency ranges. Both the Proposed and\n543\tEnd-to-end models display comparable performance,\n544\tsurpassing the Average model. Figure 4b presents L1 errors concerning the RMS of the altered signal, showing\n545\tconsistent performance across all RMS levels, despite\n546\ttraining only within the [0.02, 0.18] interval. Performance seems similar even beyond this range. Figure\n547\t4c depicts LSD relative to frequency, indicating that\n548\tProposed, Average, and End-to-end perform similarly,\n549\tthough less effectively than the Oracle condition. In\n550\tFigure 4d, LSD errors in relation to RMS reveal higher\n551\terrors at lower RMS values, even in the Oracle scenario,\n552\tunderscoring a fundamental limitation of the employed\n553\tinverse filter approach (see Eq. (5)).\n554\t5.2\n555\t\n556\tMusic Equalization and Gain Adjustment\n557\t\n558\tIn Scenario 2, musical signals were processed utilizing\n559\trandomized equalization filters and gain levels. The\n560\toutcomes, illustrated in Fig. 5 and mirroring the data\n561\tfrom Fig. 4, exhibit patterns akin to those identified in\n562\tScenario 1. Nevertheless, here, both the Average and\n563\tEnd-to-end baselines demonstrate significant performance reduction across all metrics. This deterioration\n564\tin the Average baseline’s effectiveness is probably attributable to the wider variability found in music as\n565\topposed to speech. The Proposed model sustains superior overall performance compared to the End-to-end\n566\tbaseline, suggesting that the use of pretrained CLAP\n567\tembeddings facilitates robust feature extraction, even\n568\tfor the more complex music signals. This scenario is\n569\talso evaluated through subjective listening in Sec. 6.\n570\t5.3\n571\t\n572\tNoisy Speech Equalization\n573\t\n574\tThis scenario investigates the impact of background\n575\tnoise on inverse filter estimation by analyzing the relationship between the objective metrics and the input\n576\tSNR. Figure 6a presents the L1 error of the estimated\n577\t\n578\tAutomatic Audio Equalization\n579\t\n580\tparameters as a function of SNR. Both the Proposed\n581\tand End-to-end methods exhibit similar performance,\n582\twith a noticeable but relatively minor increase in error\n583\tat lower SNRs, which aligns with expectations given\n584\tthe added noise.\n585\tThe LSD metric, defined between audio waveforms, is\n586\taffected by the speech enhancement model used for preprocessing. In Fig. 6b, we evaluate performance using\n587\tDeepFilterNet2 [25] for speech enhancement prior to\n588\tequalization. Here, all methods exhibit similar trends,\n589\theavily influenced by the SNR. To explore an idealized\n590\tscenario, we apply the estimated equalization (derived\n591\tfrom noisy measurements) to a noiseless version of\n592\tthe degraded signal, simulating perfect denoising while\n593\tretaining spectral coloration. As shown in Fig. 6c, this\n594\tsetup significantly improves LSD performance, achieving near-uniform results across the SNR range. In this\n595\tcase, the Proposed and Average conditions perform\n596\tcomparably, while the End-to-end baseline shows a\n597\tnoticeable decline in performance.\n598\t5.4\n599\t\n600\tReverberant Speech Equalization\n601\t\n602\tThis scenario builds upon the analysis in Sec. 5.3, shifting the focus to robustness against reverberation. We\n603\texamine how different metrics vary with the reverberation time applied to the measurements. Figure 7a shows\n604\tthe L1 parameter estimation error as a function of the\n605\treverberation time, T60 . The Proposed and End-to-end\n606\tmethods perform similarly, with a slight increase in\n607\terror at longer T60 values.\n608\tFigure 7b depicts the audio-domain LSD metric with\n609\trespect to T60 , where the equalization is applied to signals enhanced by DeepFilterNet2. As in Sec. 5.3, the\n610\tmetrics are heavily influenced by the performance of\n611\tthe speech enhancement model, overshadowing the underlying robustness of the equalization methods. To\n612\taddress this limitation, we repeat the experiment under an idealized scenario where perfect dereverberation is assumed. Specifically, the reference dry signal is equalized using the inverse filter estimated from\n613\tthe reverberant measurement. The result, presented in\n614\tFig. 7c, shows that the correlation between the metrics\n615\tand T60 becomes negligible under these conditions. In\n616\tthis idealized setup, the Proposed method outperforms\n617\tEnd-to-end and demonstrates a marginal but consistent\n618\tadvantage over the Average method in terms of LSD,\n619\tfurther highlighting its robustness to reverberation.\n620\t\n621\tAES International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio, London, UK\n622\t2025 September 8–10\n623\tPage 6 of 11\n624\t\n625\t\fMoliner, Välimäki, Drossos, and Hämäläinen\n626\t\n627\t4\n628\t2\n629\t100 200 500 1k 2k\n630\t\n631\t3\n632\t\n633\t5k 10k 20k\n634\t\n635\t0.1\n636\t\n637\tFrequency (Hz)\n638\t(a) L1 error vs frequency\n639\t\n640\tProposed\n641\tAverage\n642\tOracle\n643\tEnd-to-end\n644\t\n645\t2\n646\